{
  "cards_type": "object",
  "cards_length": 1,
  "first": [
    {
      "key": "excerpts",
      "value": [
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_001",
          "text": "There is a strain in demanding that a central authority override deliberately obstructive national rulebooks while insisting the same authority must never steer which technologies or products succeed."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_002",
          "text": "Where an identical product costs far more in a lower-income member country, public funds poured into buildings rather than into household buying power leave those consumers no better defended against rising prices."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_003",
          "text": "Treating regulatory certainty as a security asset implies predictability deserves the same urgency as defence or supply questions, because an economy unable to plan cannot absorb external shocks."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_004",
          "text": "If reducing regulatory volume and instability does not improve investment and productivity relative to jurisdictions retaining heavier burdens, the speaker's causal diagnosis would lose support."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_005",
          "text": "Calling energy strategic is inconsistent with allowing project delivery to take a decade and prices to remain opaque, while regional firms also struggle to obtain finance."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_006",
          "text": "An external auditor's verdict that existing rules go unenforced undermines any claim that fresh legislation is the remedy, since months spent preparing for compliance are months not spent creating value."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_007",
          "text": "If unnecessary internal barriers were removed without weakening social protections, competitiveness could improve without sacrificing the quality of life the speaker treats as the continent's advantage."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_008",
          "text": "Choosing to route around obstacles rather than remove them is an admission that direct dismantling has failed after decades, and it leaves the underlying barriers untouched for whoever cannot use the detour."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_009",
          "text": "A flat public denial that a problem exists cannot be reconciled with the same figure's earlier pledge to act against it, so one statement must give way."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_010",
          "text": "A widening gap between what is bought abroad and what is sold there suggests openness is advancing faster than domestic capacity can adjust, so further liberalisation contradicts the stated aim of building a common market."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_011",
          "text": "Insisting the project remains unfinished conveniently shields its architects from judgement, since freedoms of movement operate widely today and the affluence they were meant to deliver never arrived."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_012",
          "text": "A published roadmap reveals actual priorities far more reliably than a speech does, so the absence of decent employment from that document is evidence the spoken commitment was mainly rhetorical."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_013",
          "text": "Because an unregulated market rewards the strongest and weakens social cohesion, common rules are presented as necessary to keep economic integration compatible with democratic resilience."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_014",
          "text": "Judging this term by whether obstacles are fewer at its close than at its start is meaningful only if national administrations act, since most of the remaining work lies beyond the executive's own reach."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_015",
          "text": "If prosperity historically depended on keeping political power and business ownership apart, then decades of favouritism toward rulers' associates would have damaged not only that country's firms but its trading partners too."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_016",
          "text": "Shielding tired incumbents from rivalry cannot produce world-class firms; the harder route is funding shared infrastructure and training while letting the least efficient middlemen be squeezed out."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_017",
          "text": "An agreement settled between institutions before this chamber was properly involved begins with a legitimacy deficit, and it will ultimately be judged not by its wording but by measurable outcomes."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_018",
          "text": "Requiring a narrow technological transition while domestic producers alone bear carbon costs undermines the manufacturing base needed to supply that transition."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_019",
          "text": "When heads of government endorse a goal that their own ministers then resist, the obstacle is not absent conviction but a breakdown of coordination inside each national administration."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_020",
          "text": "If common policies systematically disadvantage less wealthy regions and concentrate capital in larger economies, integration cannot deliver the equal conditions the speaker demands."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_021",
          "text": "Neighbouring governments that block the missing connection year after year shift the burden onto households far down the line, who bear inflated bills for a failure not their own."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_022",
          "text": "Standards that no authority has the capacity to verify function as no standards at all, which makes inspection capacity rather than stricter drafting the decisive variable for fair competition."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_023",
          "text": "Adopting only the convenient half of an advisory report invites the suspicion that the exercise was never balanced, and workers who cannot cover living costs will not supply the effort competitiveness requires."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_024",
          "text": "Buying military equipment separately on incompatible systems wastes the one advantage that size confers, so larger budgets without joint purchasing mostly inflate duplication instead of delivering usable capability."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_025",
          "text": "Letting firms opt out of national company law into a lighter parallel code turns the stricter regime into a competitive handicap, pushing supervision, taxation and board-level worker voice steadily downward."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_026",
          "text": "Idle deposits at home coexisting with founders raising money abroad shows the shortage is of channels rather than capital, and faster external than internal service trade confirms the obstruction is homemade."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_027",
          "text": "Past versions collapsed under complexity, so the real risk is that negotiation quietly restores the paperwork the scheme was meant to abolish, leaving founders exactly where they began."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_028",
          "text": "Cohesion is better understood as convergence in real economic conditions than as transfers, since a founder in a lagging region gains nothing from grants while the rulebook remains unusable."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_029",
          "text": "A market that looks whole in the abstract but vanishes whenever one sector is examined closely leaves no foreign rival to blame for costs the members impose on themselves."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_030",
          "text": "If startups could scale under a single bloc-wide legal form and one digital compliance interface, expanding companies would no longer have to work through accumulated layers of separate bureaucratic requirements."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_031",
          "text": "If ownership of the largest firms drifts steadily toward those close to public office, smaller competitors face a tilted field, so defending them while tolerating that drift is incoherent."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_032",
          "text": "Universal access to essential services requires locally informed housing investment, while cross-border banking integration remains incomplete until deposits receive common protection."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_033",
          "text": "Confidence is the missing ingredient here: if the same activity must satisfy overlapping national add-ons, simplification without firmer enforcement of existing commitments will not lower costs at all."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_034",
          "text": "An institution that busies itself with difficulties which would not arise unaided invites the charge that it manufactures its own workload, with past command systems offered as the precedent."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_035",
          "text": "Because the remedy requires no borrowing at all, its absence cannot be explained by fiscal constraint, leaving unwillingness among governments as the only remaining explanation for the delay."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_036",
          "text": "Firms innovate where employees feel valued, so presenting decent conditions and competitiveness as rival options misreads which of the two actually produces the other."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_037",
          "text": "If those who teach and nurse cannot pay for a home within reach of their posts, vacancies follow, making shelter costs an economic constraint rather than a separate welfare matter."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_038",
          "text": "If one reporting portal and mutual recognition replaced separate national procedures, delivery would begin restoring the trust that repeated unfulfilled promises have depleted."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_039",
          "text": "Member-state refusal even to simplify and digitise routine posting procedures indicates that political resistance, rather than lack of known solutions, is blocking progress."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_040",
          "text": "Since the shortfall lies in fragmentation rather than in productive capacity, the case for deeper integration is strongest exactly where cross-border supply chains already operate daily and would notice any friction immediately."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_041",
          "text": "A chamber whose central economic debate cannot hold the attention of the executive's head has a procedural problem before an economic one, and ceremonial business crowding it out compounds that."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_042",
          "text": "Because small firms devote productive time to paperwork while large corporations can hire specialist advisers, additional complexity gives larger competitors a structural advantage."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_043",
          "text": "Demanding predictable rules while repeatedly changing energy policy is inconsistent, and sacrificing a reliable generating source weakens the competitiveness that regulatory stability was meant to support."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_044",
          "text": "A continent-wide company form will only earn acceptance if it carries floors on pay and participation; otherwise people experience integration as something imposed on them and turn away."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_045",
          "text": "Progress would be demonstrated only if the known barriers were actually removed and the executive could credibly report completion, rather than repeating another diagnosis."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_046",
          "text": "Existing talent and household savings mean fragmentation and political timidity, not shortages of people or money, are the obstacles to stronger firms and the benefits they support."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_047",
          "text": "If living standards improved materially after a government took office, that outcome would weaken claims that its economic record was a failure."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_048",
          "text": "Language promising stability for investors translates on the shop floor into longer and more elastic hours, so one word cannot describe an improvement for both sides at once."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_049",
          "text": "Endorsing at one level the labour reforms you deplore at another is untenable, and a called general strike is evidence that those affected do not accept the distinction between levels."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_050",
          "text": "Announcing ever more sectoral unions without finishing any of them dilutes credibility, so committing to one dated horizon for completing them all would test whether the intention is serious."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_051",
          "text": "Simplification becomes deregulation only when protections are removed; if national authorities instead stop adding extra layers, paperwork can fall while common standards remain intact."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_052",
          "text": "Calling a defensive veto short-sighted implies the anticipated losses are smaller than what is forgone, which is precisely the calculation a national leader is least willing to hear stated aloud."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_053",
          "text": "Productivity presupposes people who are healthy and trained, so relegating the social dimension to the final item of a list inverts the order in which competitiveness is actually built."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_054",
          "text": "A market that moves goods and money freely while a growing share of those in work remain poor has met only half its purpose, and the gap will widen without deliberate correction."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_055",
          "text": "Judged as an instrument rather than a destination, integration should be assessed by whether it narrows gaps in taxation, wages and the cost of heating a home for ordinary households."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_056",
          "text": "Demanding standards of domestic producers that imported goods need not meet is a handicap rather than competition, and today's convenient supplier can become tomorrow's leverage against you."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_057",
          "text": "When responsibility for one site is split among scores of contractors, nobody is accountable for safety, so awarding work at the lowest price predictably converts cost savings into deaths."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_058",
          "text": "If workers could use their qualifications anywhere and founders could raise capital from investors elsewhere within the bloc, leaving for opportunity would become less necessary."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_059",
          "text": "When many firms benefit from market access yet increasingly report bureaucracy as a barrier to growth, preserving openness requires reducing the administrative burden."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_060",
          "text": "Because assembly work and supplier networks straddle a border, energy costs decided elsewhere squeeze both partners at once, which makes any purely national defence of industry impossible."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_061",
          "text": "Because governments lose reform momentum after accession, completing integration requires member states to renew the willingness to surrender national protections that entry once demanded."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_062",
          "text": "Cutting paperwork for employers cannot mean cutting protection for staff, because new workplace technologies and layered subcontracting create fresh risks precisely where oversight is being thinned."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_063",
          "text": "That the most common destination for departing graduates lies inside the bloc shows integration works, yet uneven development turns free movement into an obligation rather than a genuine choice."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_064",
          "text": "A trading bloc's openness ultimately rests on the people staffing its quays and checkpoints, because compliant producers lose whenever unchecked consignments reach buyers more cheaply than properly certified stock."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_065",
          "text": "Uniform labour rules would, in the speaker's view, spread overregulation across every member state, while preserving national differences allows regulatory competition."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_066",
          "text": "That agreement comes quickly when an outside counterpart sits opposite yet stalls when the same parties face each other shows the capacity for compromise exists and is simply not applied internally."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_067",
          "text": "If each remaining barrier were assigned publicly to the government responsible, representatives could apply targeted pressure at home instead of debating obstacles only in general terms."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_068",
          "text": "Choosing directly applicable instruments over those needing national transposition removes the discretion that generates divergence, yet much of what still blocks firms can be repaired only by national administrations themselves."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_069",
          "text": "Preferring what is made at home across the whole union turns trade policy into a shield, and that holds only if the jobs preserved are decent enough to justify the project."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_070",
          "text": "A parallel rulebook that sits alongside national ones only helps if firms actually choose it, so its value depends less on drafting than on whether cross-border founders find it simpler."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_071",
          "text": "A group that respects sectoral bargaining in its home market while refusing it in a foreign subsidiary shows the difference lies in what local law permits, not in what the business needs."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_072",
          "text": "The lever for removing barriers already exists in enforcement powers, so declining to use them makes the shortage of risk capital a symptom rather than the primary cause of stalled growth."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_073",
          "text": "If living standards have slipped behind places once considered poor, the argument concludes that judgement about enterprise belongs with those who run it rather than with appointed administrators."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_074",
          "text": "Freedom to ship products means little when the networks carrying power, data and finance stop at each frontier, and young firms therefore run out of money long before they run out of ideas."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_075",
          "text": "Adding a poorly understood and distrusted payment instrument compounds regulatory complexity, while allowing different speeds of integration risks further fragmentation."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_076",
          "text": "If registering once and notifying once were enough, the paperwork accompanying a posted crew would shrink to a formality, and every new law ought therefore to be costed first against a small firm."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_077",
          "text": "Weakening the bodies that inspect workplaces while accident numbers climb reveals whose interests the arrangement actually budgets for, since supervision is the one cost only employers benefit from cutting."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_078",
          "text": "A project that asked to be judged by the prosperity it promised must accept that same test, and distant climate projections carry little authority from forecasters who miss the coming week."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_079",
          "text": "Economic size becomes leverage abroad only if qualifications and finance move freely at home, and scattering related files across separate committees risks producing rules that contradict one another."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_080",
          "text": "An economic space large enough to set terms becomes a liability when it declines to use that weight, so openness without a strategy hands the advantage to whoever does have one."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_081",
          "text": "Since no external obstacle prevents a national treasury from moving first, continued inaction is a choice, and honesty would require admitting the objective is not genuinely shared."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_082",
          "text": "Universal agreement in principle combined with persistent blockage in practice implies the obstruction is deliberate, protecting particular domestic sectors whose losses would be concentrated while the gains stay diffuse."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_083",
          "text": "Withdrawing obligations after some businesses have already invested in meeting them punishes preparation and rewards delay, teaching every board that waiting out a requirement is the cheaper strategy."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_084",
          "text": "Financing projects abroad without designing tenders so domestic suppliers can compete means public money generates growth elsewhere, a self-defeating outcome that no amount of internal deregulation would correct."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_085",
          "text": "When talented founders seek financing abroad, incomplete capital-market integration rather than lack of ability is the constraint, and adding new internal obstacles would leave it untouched."
        },
        {
          "excerpt_id": "excerpt_086",
          "text": "Strategy documents cannot lower the electricity bill that closes a factory, and an online space where buyers enjoy no effective redress shows the promised certainty exists mainly on paper."
        }
      ]
    }
  ]
}

